<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> What does the lesson mean by the "sensory interconnections" of sculpture?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">A sculpture must be touched to be understood.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Looking at a sculpture lets you sense its weight, coolness, or roughness as if you could touch it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">All sculptures are made of the same material.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Sculpture combines sound and sight.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Sculpture engages sight and, at least imaginatively, touch. You sense the marble's smoothness or the bronze's roughness without laying a hand on it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about how a sculpture's material comes across through sight alone. The interconnection is between seeing and imagined touch.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Which sculptural technique builds up material, such as clay or wax?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Printing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Casting.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Carving.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Modeling.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Modeling adds and shapes material. Carving removes it, casting pours molten metal into a mold, and construction assembles separate pieces.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Carving removes material from a block, and casting pours molten material into a mold. The technique that builds material up is modeling.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> In high relief, figures</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">are carved into a surface and do not project at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">stand completely free of any background.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">project substantially from the background, nearly detaching from it, but remain attached to a plane.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">hang from the ceiling.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Sunken relief is carved into a surface, low relief projects slightly, and high relief projects substantially. In all three the sculpture remains attached to a plane.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Relief means the sculpture stays attached to a background. High relief projects the most, so the figures almost detach.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Why does freestanding sculpture have no single "correct" viewpoint?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Because sculptors make no decisions about viewpoint.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It is meant to be seen from multiple angles, and each angle reveals different relationships between forms.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Because only the back of a sculpture matters.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Because it is usually too small to see clearly.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. This is a fundamental difference from painting, which fixes the viewer's position.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Sculpture in the round is made to be walked around. Each angle shows something new, so no single view is the correct one.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> What is "sensory space" in sculpture?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">The empty space inside a building.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Another word for the base of a statue.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">The space around a sculpture, which is shaped by its mass and volume and can draw you in, push you back, or invite you to circle.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">The space on a museum wall.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A sculpture organizes the space around it. Paying attention to that space is essential to understanding how sculpture works.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The space around a sculpture is not empty. It is shaped by the work's mass and volume, which is why the lesson calls it sensory space.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> Why has the human body been sculpture's primary subject for thousands of years?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Because patrons only paid for portraits.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Because sculptors cannot make anything else.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Because the body is easy to carve.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">When we see a sculpted body, we project our own physical sensations onto it, so tension, a twist, or a reaching hand produces a feeling in us.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A tensed muscle or a reaching hand gives an external image for an internal feeling. That is why the body carries so much meaning.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson's argument is about response. We feel our own body in the sculpted one, which is why the body is such a powerful subject.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> Rodin's The Thinker was originally part of The Gates of Hell. How does knowing this change the content?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">The figure is not merely thinking in a generic way; he is witnessing human anguish and struggling to comprehend it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">The sculpture becomes a portrait of Rodin.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Nothing changes; the sculpture is purely abstract.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">The figure is relaxing.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The tension in the shoulders, the hand against the face, and the forward lean express the weight of moral awareness. Context enriches what the form already shows.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson's point is that background knowledge deepens perception. Here it turns generic thinking into a response to suffering.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> Why does the title of Louise Bourgeois's Maman matter?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">It tells you the spider is made of marble.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">It reveals that the huge, possibly menacing spider is a portrait of the artist's mother, who was a weaver; spiders suggest protection and patience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">It names the museum that owns the work.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">It has no effect on the meaning.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The tension between the threatening scale and the maternal subject matter is where the content lives.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The title is the key to the content. Maman means mother, and the artist connected spiders with her weaving mother.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> Calder's mobile is described as constructivist sculpture. What does that mean here?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It is assembled from separate pieces, and its composition changes continuously as the pieces move.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It is a flat relief on a wall.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">It is cast in bronze in one piece.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It is carved from a single block of marble.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A mobile challenges the idea that sculpture must be heavy, stable, and permanent: its form is a set of possibilities governed by balance, weight, and chance.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Constructivist sculpture is assembled from separate pieces. Calder's pieces hang and move, so the work is never the same twice.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> Bernini's Apollo and Daphne shows the instant Daphne begins to turn into a laurel tree. What does the sculpture ask the viewer to do?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Circle it, since the chase and the transformation are seen from different positions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Ignore the figures and look at the base.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Touch the marble.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Look at it only from the front.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A story that unfolds around the figures cannot be taken in from one position, which is exactly the strength of sculpture in the round.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about what sculpture in the round allows. Bernini put different moments of the story on different sides, so you must move.</div>
  </div>
</div>

